    "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Universal property.** For every group $G$ and every map $f:S\\to G$ with $f(s)^2=1$ for all $s\\in S$ and $(f(s)f(t))^{m(s,t)}=1$ in $G$ for all $s\\ne t$ with $m(s,t)<\\infty$, there is a unique group homomorphism $\\varphi:W\\to G$ with $\\varphi(s)=f(s)$ for all $s\\in S$ ([[def-group-homomorphism]], [[thm-quotient-group-universal-property]], [[thm-von-dyck]], [[def-group-presentation]], [[def-relators-relations-and-finite-presentations]], [[prop-equality-of-words-in-a-presentation]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**Deletion.** If the word $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ in $S$ is not reduced, then there are $i<j$ with $$s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots\\widehat{s_j}\\cdots s_k=s_1\\cdots s_k .$$ Hence repeated deletion of two letters transforms every word into a reduced expression for the same element, and a word is reduced if and only if it cannot be shortened by deleting two letters.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-hh-matsumoto-reduced-word-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**Braid moves.** Call two words in $S$ *braid-equivalent* when one is obtained from the other by finitely many replacements of an alternating subword $s\\,t\\,s\\,t\\cdots$ of length $m(s,t)<\\infty$ by the alternating word $t\\,s\\,t\\,s\\cdots$ of the same length.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-finite-symmetric-group-and-permutation-notation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **symmetric group on $n$ letters** is $$S_n:=\\operatorname{Sym}(n)=\\operatorname{Sym}(\\{0,1,\\dots,n-1\\}),$$ the group of all bijections of $n$ under composition ([[def-symmetric-group]]), with the composition convention $$(\\sigma\\tau)(i):=(\\sigma\\circ\\tau)(i)=\\sigma(\\tau(i))\\qquad(i\\in n),$$ so that in a product the right-hand factor acts first.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-inversions-inversion-number-and-sign",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **inversion** of $\\sigma$ is a pair $(i,j)$ with $i<j<n$ and $\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(j)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-disjoint-cycles-commute",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Cycles with disjoint supports commute.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-adjacent-transpositions-generate-the-symmetric-group",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $n\\ge2$, $S_n$ is generated by the adjacent transpositions $s_j=(j\\ j+1)$, $1\\le j<n$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-coset",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $g\\in G$, the **left coset** and **right coset** of $H$ represented by $g$ are $$gH:=\\{gh:h\\in H\\},\\qquad Hg:=\\{hg:h\\in H\\}.$$ The element $g$ is a **representative** of these cosets.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-well-ordering-principle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every nonempty subset $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ has a least element: there is $\\ell \\in S$ with $\\ell \\le s$ for all $s \\in S$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "1.1",
          "claim": "**Support and Tits reduction.** Let $w\\in W$. If $s_1\\cdots s_k$ and $s'_1\\cdots s'_k$ are reduced expressions of $w$, then by [F3] they are braid-equivalent, and a braid move replaces an alternating block in two letters by the other alternating word of the same length, so it preserves the set of letters occurring; hence the set $S(w)$ of letters of a reduced expression is independent of the chosen expression. Next, let $t_1\\cdots t_r$ be a word in $S$ with value $w$ and length $r>\\ell(w)$; by [F2] there are indices $i<j$ with $t_1\\cdots\\widehat{t_i}\\cdots\\widehat{t_j}\\cdots t_r=t_1\\cdots t_r=w$, so deleting the two letters leaves the value and uses only letters already present. Iterating, the length drops by two at each step until it reaches $\\ell(w)$, and the resulting reduced expression of $w$ uses only letters of the original word. Consequently $w\\in W_J$ if and only if $S(w)\\subseteq J$: if $S(w)\\subseteq J$, a reduced expression of $w$ is a word in the letters of $J$, so $w\\in\\langle J\\rangle=W_J$; conversely, if $w\\in W_J$, then $w$ is a product of elements of $J$, hence the value of some word in the letters $J$, which reduces as above to a reduced expression whose letters lie in $J$, so $S(w)\\subseteq J$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "1.2",
          "claim": "**Type A.** Let $n\\ge2$ and let $W$ now be the group presented by the type-A matrix on $S=\\{s_1,\\dots,s_{n-1}\\}$; we use the library's symmetric group $S_n=\\operatorname{Sym}(\\{0,\\dots,n-1\\})$ and the transpositions $\\tau_i=(i\\ i+1)$ for $0\\le i\\le n-2$, which are the elements written $(i+1\\ i+2)$ in the statement under the order-preserving identification of $\\{0,\\dots,n-1\\}$ with $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$. The relators hold in $S_n$: $\\tau_i^2=\\operatorname{id}$; $\\tau_i\\tau_j=\\tau_j\\tau_i$ when $|i-j|>1$, because the two transpositions have disjoint supports and disjoint cycles commute by [F6]; and $\\tau_i\\tau_{i+1}\\tau_i=\\tau_{i+1}\\tau_i\\tau_{i+1}$ by direct computation on the three symbols $i,i+1,i+2$. By the universal property in [F1] there is a homomorphism $\\varphi:W\\to S_n$ with $\\varphi(s_{i+1})=\\tau_i$, and it is onto because the adjacent transpositions generate $S_n$ by [F6]. We show $|W|\\le n!$. Let $H:=\\langle s_1,\\dots,s_{n-2}\\rangle\\le W$ (so $H=\\{1\\}$ when $n=2$), and for $1\\le j\\le n-1$ put $r_j:=s_{n-1}s_{n-2}\\cdots s_j$, and put $r_n:=1$. Right multiplication by a generator obeys: if $i\\le j-2$ then $r_js_i=s_ir_j$, because $s_i$ commutes with every factor of $r_j$; if $i=j-1$ then $r_js_i=r_{j-1}$; if $i=j$ then $r_js_i=r_{j+1}$, by $s_j^2=1$ (with $r_{n-1}s_{n-1}=r_n$); and if $i>j$ then $r_js_i=s_{i-1}r_j$, because moving the final $s_i$ left past $s_{i-2},\\dots,s_j$ and using $s_is_{i-1}s_i=s_{i-1}s_is_{i-1}$ turns it into a leading $s_{i-1}$, which then commutes left past $s_{i+1},\\dots,s_{n-1}$; for $r_n=1$ the same rules read $r_ns_i=s_ir_n$ for $i\\le n-2$ and $r_ns_{n-1}=r_{n-1}$. In each case $r_js_i$ has the form $h\\,r_k$ with $h\\in H$: either $h=s_i$ with $i\\le n-2$, or $h=1$, or $h=s_{i-1}$ with $i-1\\le n-2$. Hence the union $T=\\bigcup_{j=1}^n Hr_j$ contains $1=r_n$ and is stable under right multiplication by every generator; since every generator is its own inverse, every word in the generators lies in $T$, so $W=T$ and $|W|\\le n\\,|H|$. The relators of the type-A matrix on $n-2$ generators hold among $s_1,\\dots,s_{n-2}$ in $W$, so by [F1] there is a surjection from the corresponding presented group onto $H$; by induction on $n$, whose base $n=2$ gives $|W|=2$, this yields $|H|\\le(n-1)!$ and hence $|W|\\le n!$. On the other hand $|S_n|=n!$, since a bijection of the $n$-element set $\\{0,\\dots,n-1\\}$ is determined by choosing the image of $0$ in $n$ ways, then the image of $1$ in $n-1$ ways, and so on. Therefore the surjection $\\varphi$ between the finite groups $W$ and $S_n$ is a bijection, hence an isomorphism. It remains to identify $\\ell$ with the inversion number. Right multiplication by $\\tau_i$ interchanges the entries at positions $i$ and $i+1$ in the one-line notation by [F4], so it exchanges the inversion statuses of the pairs $(p,i),(p,i+1)$ for $p<i$ and of the pairs $(i,q),(i+1,q)$ for $q>i+1$, while the pair $(i,i+1)$ becomes an inversion exactly when it was not one; hence $\\operatorname{inv}(\\sigma\\tau_i)=\\operatorname{inv}(\\sigma)\\pm1$, with $\\operatorname{inv}(\\sigma\\tau_i)=\\operatorname{inv}(\\sigma)-1$ exactly when $\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(i+1)$ by [F5]. Therefore every word of length $k$ in the generators $\\tau_i$ representing $\\sigma$ has $\\operatorname{inv}(\\sigma)\\le k$ (each letter changes the inversion number by one), so $\\operatorname{inv}(\\sigma)\\le\\ell_A(\\sigma)$ for the length function $\\ell_A$ of $S_n$ with respect to the generating set $\\{\\tau_i\\}$; and if $\\operatorname{inv}(\\sigma)>0$ then some $i$ has $\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(i+1)$ (otherwise $\\sigma$ would be increasing, hence the identity), so $\\ell_A(\\sigma)\\le\\ell_A(\\sigma\\tau_i)+1=\\operatorname{inv}(\\sigma\\tau_i)+1=\\operatorname{inv}(\\sigma)$ by induction on the inversion number, giving $\\ell_A=\\operatorname{inv}$. Finally $\\ell(w)=\\ell_A(\\varphi(w))$ for all $w\\in W$: a word of length $\\ell(w)$ in the $s_i$ representing $w$ maps to a word of the same length in the $\\tau_i$ representing $\\varphi(w)$, so $\\ell_A(\\varphi(w))\\le\\ell(w)$; conversely a word $\\tau_{i_1}\\cdots\\tau_{i_k}$ of length $k=\\ell_A(\\varphi(w))$ for $\\varphi(w)$ lifts to the word $s_{i_1+1}\\cdots s_{i_k+1}$, which maps to $\\varphi(w)$, so it represents $w$ by injectivity of $\\varphi$ and $\\ell(w)\\le k$. Hence $\\ell(w)=\\operatorname{inv}(\\varphi(w))$, and by [F2] a word in the $s_i$ is reduced exactly when its length equals the inversion number of its value.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "2.1",
          "claim": "**The intrinsic parabolic presentation.** Let $W_J^\\ast$ be the group presented by $(J,m|_J)$ as in [F1]. The map $f:J\\to W_J\\le W$, $j\\mapsto j$, satisfies the relator conditions in $W$, because every relator of the restricted matrix is a relator of $(S,m)$; so [F1] gives a homomorphism $f:W_J^\\ast\\to W_J$, which is onto because the elements of $J$ generate $W_J$. For injectivity define $f':W_J\\to W_J^\\ast$ by choosing, for $w\\in W_J$, a reduced expression $w=j_1\\cdots j_q$ in $W$ and setting $f'(w):=j_1\\cdots j_q$ (product in $W_J^\\ast$); by step 1.1 all letters $j_l$ lie in $J$. This is well defined: another reduced expression $j'_1\\cdots j'_q$ of $w$ is braid-equivalent to it by [F3], and every braid move involved replaces an alternating block in two letters of $J$ by the other alternating word, which is a defining relation of $W_J^\\ast$, so the two products agree. To see that $f'$ is a homomorphism it suffices to show $f'(jw)=j\\,f'(w)$ for $j\\in J$ and $w\\in W_J$. If $\\ell(jw)=\\ell(w)+1$, then prepending $j$ to a reduced expression of $w$ gives a reduced expression of $jw$ with letters in $J$, and the claim is immediate. If $\\ell(jw)=\\ell(w)-1$, then [F2] applied to a reduced expression $w=j_1\\cdots j_q$ gives $jw=j_1\\cdots\\widehat{j_i}\\cdots j_q$ for some $i$. The deleted word is reduced of length $q-1$, and prepending $j$ to it gives another reduced expression of $w$ of length $q$, with every letter in $J$. By [F3] these two reduced expressions of $w$ are braid-equivalent using only letters in $J$, so their products agree in $W_J^\\ast$: $j_1\\cdots j_q=j\\,j_1\\cdots\\widehat{j_i}\\cdots j_q$. Multiplying this equality by $j$ in $W_J^\\ast$ gives $j\\,f'(w)=j_1\\cdots\\widehat{j_i}\\cdots j_q=f'(jw)$. Iterating $f'(jw)=j f'(w)$ along a word for any $u\\in W_J$, and using $f'(1)=1$, gives $f'(uw)=f'(u)f'(w)$, so $f'$ is a homomorphism. By construction $ff'(w)=w$ for all $w\\in W_J$, and $f'f$ fixes each generator of $W_J^\\ast$ because $f'(j)=j$ for $j\\in J$; hence $f'f=\\mathrm{id}_{W_J^\\ast}$. Thus $f$ and $f'$ are inverse isomorphisms, and $(W_J,J)$ is a Coxeter system. If $w\\in W_J$ and $j_1\\cdots j_q$ is a reduced expression of $w$ in $W$, then all $j_l\\in J$ by step 1.1, so the same word of length $q$ is a word in the generators $J$ of $W_J^\\ast$, giving $\\ell_J(w)\\le\\ell(w)$; conversely a word in the letters $J$ representing $w$ is a word in $S$ representing $w$, so $\\ell(w)\\le\\ell_J(w)$; hence $\\ell_J=\\ell$ on $W_J$. If $s\\in S\\cap W_J$, then $S(s)=\\{s\\}\\subseteq J$ by step 1.1, so $s\\in J$ and $W_J\\cap S=J$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "3.1",
          "claim": "**Minimal coset representatives.** Fix $a\\in W$. The set $\\{\\ell(x):x\\in W_Ja\\}$ is a nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{N}$ and so has a least element by [F8]; choose $d\\in W_Ja$ of minimal length. For $s\\in J$ one has $sd\\in W_Ja$, so $\\ell(sd)\\ge\\ell(d)$; by [F2] $\\ell(sd)=\\ell(d)\\pm1$, and therefore $\\ell(sd)=\\ell(d)+1>\\ell(d)$. Now let $u\\in W_J$ with a reduced expression $u=u_1\\cdots u_p$ and let $d=d_1\\cdots d_q$ be reduced, so $q=\\ell(d)$; by step 1.1 all $u_l$ lie in $J$. Applying the Tits reduction of step 1.1 to the concatenated word $u_1\\cdots u_pd_1\\cdots d_q$ produces a reduced expression of $ud$ that is a subsequence of it, hence splits as $u'\\,d'$ with $u'$ a subsequence of $u_1\\cdots u_p$ and $d'$ a subsequence of $d_1\\cdots d_q$; write $u',d'$ also for their values. If $d'$ is not the full word $d_1\\cdots d_q$, then $d'=(u')^{-1}ud\\in W_Jd=W_Ja$, while $d'$ has fewer than $q$ letters, so $\\ell(d')<q=\\ell(d)$, contradicting the minimality of $d$. Hence $d'$ is the full $d$-word, so $u'd=ud$ and $u'=u$; since $u'$ is a subsequence of the reduced word $u_1\\cdots u_p$ of length $p=\\ell(u)$ and represents $u$, it must use all $p$ letters, so the concatenation is reduced and $\\ell(ud)=p+q=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$. If $d''\\in W_Ja$ also has minimal length, write $d''=ud$ with $u\\in W_J$; then $\\ell(d'')=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$ with $\\ell(d'')=\\ell(d)$, so $\\ell(u)=0$, $u=1$ and $d''=d$; this gives uniqueness. Conversely, let $d$ satisfy $\\ell(sd)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$ and write $d=u\\,d_0$ with $d_0$ the minimal representative of $W_Jd$; additivity gives $\\ell(d)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d_0)$, and if $u\\neq1$ and $u=s_1\\cdots s_p$ is a reduced expression with $p\\ge1$, then $\\ell(s_1d)=\\ell(s_2\\cdots s_pd_0)\\le(p-1)+\\ell(d_0)<\\ell(d)$, contradicting the hypothesis; hence $u=1$ and $d=d_0$ is the minimal representative. The factorization $w=ud$ of an arbitrary $w\\in W$ is now obtained by taking $d$ minimal in $W_Jw$ and $u:=wd^{-1}\\in W_J$, and its uniqueness follows from the uniqueness of $d$. For right cosets, note that $\\ell(u^{-1})=\\ell(u)$ for every $u$: reversing a reduced word for $u$ gives a word of the same length for $u^{-1}$, so $\\ell(u^{-1})\\le\\ell(u)$, and applying this to $u^{-1}$ gives equality. Inversion is an anti-automorphism interchanging left and right cosets and fixing lengths, so applying the left-coset statement to inverses gives unique minimal elements of the right cosets $aW_J$, characterized by $\\ell(ds)>\\ell(d)$ for $s\\in J$ and satisfying $\\ell(du)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(u)$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
