    "ex-hh-finite-dihedral-reduced-words": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Define $$W:=F(S)/N,$$ and write $s$ (as well as $sN$) for the image of $s\\in S$ in $W$ ([[def-quotient-group]], [[def-group]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. **Involutions.** $\\sigma_s^2=\\mathrm{id}_E$ for every $s\\in S$; each $\\sigma_s$ is invertible, and $\\ell(s)=1$.\n2. **The rank-two block.** For any distinct $s,t\\in S$, put $m:=m(s,t)$, $c:=c_{st}$, $P:=\\operatorname{span}(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)\\subseteq E$, $A:=\\sigma_s\\sigma_t$ and $B:=A|_P$. Then $A(P)\\subseteq P$, and in the basis $(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)$,\n$$B(\\alpha_s)=(c^2-1)\\alpha_s+c\\,\\alpha_t,\\qquad B(\\alpha_t)=-c\\,\\alpha_s-\\alpha_t;\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(B)=c^2-2,\\qquad \\det(B)=1 .$$\nMoreover $A(v)-v\\in P$ for every $v\\in E$. Consequently $E=P\\oplus Q$ with $Q:=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\alpha_u:u\\in S,\\ u\\notin\\{s,t\\}\\}$, and for every $v=p+q$ with $p\\in P$, $q\\in Q$ and every $k\\ge1$,\n$$A^k(p+q)=B^k p+\\bigl(\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{k-1}\\bigr)\\bigl(A(q)-q\\bigr)+q .$$\n3. **Exact order of the rank-two product.** For any distinct $s,t$ and the notation of (2), when $m<\\infty$ put $\\zeta:=\\zeta_{st}$, of order $2m$, so $c=\\zeta+\\zeta^{-1}$; when $m=\\infty$ use $c=2$, with no $\\zeta$ stipulated. Then:\n   (a) if $2<m<\\infty$, the characteristic polynomial of $B$ is $X^2-(c^2-2)X+1=(X-\\zeta^2)(X-\\zeta^{-2})$ with distinct roots; $B$ is diagonalisable, $B^m=\\mathrm{id}_P$, $\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{m-1}=0$, and $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for $0<k<m$;\n   (b) if $m=2$, then $c=0$ and $B=-\\mathrm{id}_P$;\n   (c) if $m=\\infty$, then $c=2$, $B\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$, $(B-\\mathrm{id}_P)^2=0$, and $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for every $k\\ge1$.\n   Hence $\\sigma_s\\sigma_t=A$ has order exactly $m$ when $m<\\infty$, and infinite order when $m=\\infty$; in particular $A^m=\\mathrm{id}_E$ and $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$ for $0<k<m$ in the finite case.\n4. **The representation and the exact dihedral orders.** The assignment $s\\mapsto\\sigma_s$ respects every defining relator of $W$ and induces a unique homomorphism $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ with $\\sigma(s)=\\sigma_s$. Consequently, for any distinct $s,t\\in S$, one has $s\\ne t$ in $W$ and $st$ has order exactly $m(s,t)$ in $W$ (infinite when $m(s,t)=\\infty$).",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**Ambient reducedness of dihedral words.** Let $s\\ne t$, $m:=m(s,t)$, and for $q\\ge1$ let $w_q$ be the value of the alternating word of length $q$ beginning with $s$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**Deletion.** If the word $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ in $S$ is not reduced, then there are $i<j$ with $$s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots\\widehat{s_j}\\cdots s_k=s_1\\cdots s_k .$$ Hence repeated deletion of two letters transforms every word into a reduced expression for the same element, and a word is reduced if and only if it cannot be shortened by deleting two letters.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-group-power",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "By the recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]), applied with the set $M$, the element $e$ and the function $x \\mapsto x \\cdot g$ from $M$ to $M$, there is exactly one function $\\mathbb{N} \\to M$, written $n \\mapsto g^{n}$, with $$g^{0} = e, \\qquad g^{\\sigma(n)} = g^{n} \\cdot g \\quad (n \\in \\mathbb{N}).$$ In particular $g^{0} = e$ for **every** $g$, including $g = e$, and $g^{1} = g^{\\sigma(0)} = e \\cdot g = g$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-order-in-a-group",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "If $S_g \\ne \\varnothing$, the **order of $g$** is its least element, $$\\operatorname{ord}(g) \\;:=\\; \\min S_g \\;\\in\\; \\mathbb{N},$$ which exists by the well-ordering principle ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]): every nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{N}$ has a least element, and that element is unique, being $\\le$ every element of $S_g$ and a member of it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "1.1",
          "claim": "**Exhaustion.** Every element of $W$ is the value of a word in $s,t$ whose inverse letters are again letters ($s^{-1}=s$, $t^{-1}=t$ in $W$ because $s^2,t^2\\in R$ [F1]), so it suffices to treat words in $s,t$. Cancelling all consecutive equal letters using $s^2=t^2=1$ turns any such word into an alternating word $(u_1,\\dots,u_q)$ with $u_{i+1}\\ne u_i$. If $q$ is even, the value of the alternating word beginning with $s$ is $(st)^{q/2}$ and that of the one beginning with $t$ is $(ts)^{q/2}=(st)^{-q/2}$ [F5]; if $q$ is odd, the corresponding values are $(st)^{(q-1)/2}s$ and $(ts)^{(q-1)/2}t=(st)^{-(q-1)/2}t=(st)^{-(q+1)/2}s$, because $t=(st)^{-1}s$. Since $(st)^m=1$ [F1], an integer power $(st)^j$ equals $(st)^{k}$ for the unique $k\\in\\{0,\\dots,m-1\\}$ with $j\\equiv k\\pmod m$, and $(st)^js=(st)^ks$; hence every element of $W$ is one of the $2m$ listed elements $(st)^k,(st)^ks$ with $0\\le k<m$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "1.2",
          "claim": "**Reducedness of alternating words.** Let $1\\le q\\le m$ and let $w_q$ be the value of the alternating word of length $q$ beginning with $s$; by [F3], $\\ell(w_q)=q$ and every word in $S$ representing $w_q$ has length at least $q$, so that alternating word is reduced, and $w_1,\\dots,w_q$ are pairwise distinct. The same statements hold for alternating words beginning with $t$: interchanging the roles of $s$ and $t$ preserves every hypothesis, since $m(t,s)=m(s,t)=m$ and the presentation is symmetric in $s,t$ [F1].",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "2.1",
          "claim": "**Distinctness.** If $(st)^k=(st)^l$ with $0\\le k<l<m$, then $(st)^{l-k}=1$ with $0<l-k<m$, contradicting the exact order $m$ of $st$ [F2, F5]. If $(st)^ks=(st)^ls$ with $0\\le k<l<m$, then multiplying on the right by $s$ gives $(st)^k=(st)^l$, the previous case. If finally $(st)^k=(st)^ls$, then $(st)^{k-l}=s$, so $s\\in\\langle st\\rangle$ and also $t=s\\cdot st\\in\\langle st\\rangle$, whence $W=\\langle st\\rangle$ is cyclic and therefore abelian; then $st=ts$, so $(st)^2=stst=s(ts)t=s(st)t=(ss)(tt)=1$, and the order of $st$ divides $2$. Since that order is $m\\ge2$ by [F2], this forces $m=2$, in which case $\\langle st\\rangle=\\{1,st\\}$ while $s\\in\\{1,st\\}$ gives $s=1$ or $t=1$; both are impossible because $\\ell(s)=\\ell(t)=1$ [F2]. Hence no rotation equals a reflection and the $2m$ elements of step 1.1 are pairwise distinct, so they exhaust $W$ and $|W|=2m$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "2.2",
          "claim": "**The rotation lengths.** For $0\\le k\\le m$ the element $(st)^k$ also equals $(ts)^{m-k}$: indeed $(ts)=(st)^{-1}$ [F5], so $(ts)^{m-k}=(st)^{-(m-k)}=(st)^{k-m}=(st)^k$, using $(st)^m=1$ [F1]. The two expressions $(st)^k$ and $(ts)^{m-k}$ are alternating words of lengths $2k$ and $2(m-k)$, whose minimum $q_0:=\\min(2k,2(m-k))$ satisfies $q_0\\le m$ because the two lengths sum to $2m$. The shorter of the two words has length $q_0$; if $q_0=0$ it is the empty word with value $1$, so $\\ell((st)^k)=0=q_0$, and if $q_0\\ge1$ it is an alternating word of length $q_0\\le m$ whose value is $(st)^k$, so step 1.2 gives $\\ell((st)^k)=q_0$ and shows that no word represents $(st)^k$ with fewer than $q_0$ letters. Hence $\\ell((st)^k)=\\min(2k,2(m-k))$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "3.1",
          "claim": "**The reflection lengths.** For $0\\le k\\le m-1$ one has $(st)^ks=(ts)^{m-k}s=(ts)^{m-k-1}(ts)s=(ts)^{m-k-1}t$, because $(ts)^{m-k}=(st)^{k-m}=(st)^k$ as in step 2.2; these are alternating words of lengths $2k+1$ and $2(m-k)-1$, whose minimum $q_1:=\\min(2k+1,2(m-k)-1)$ is at most $m$: indeed $q_1\\le 2k+1$ and $q_1\\le 2(m-k)-1$, so $2q_1\\le 2m$, that is $q_1\\le m$. The shorter word is nonempty because $q_1\\ge1$ for $0\\le k\\le m-1$, and step 1.2 applied to it (with the roles of $s$ and $t$ interchanged if it begins with $t$) gives that it is reduced, that its value $(st)^ks$ has length exactly $q_1$, and that no word for $(st)^ks$ is shorter. Hence $\\ell\\bigl((st)^ks\\bigr)=\\min(2k+1,2(m-k)-1)$; the endpoint $k=0$ gives $\\ell(s)=\\min(1,2m-1)=1$ and the endpoint $k=m-1$ gives $\\ell\\bigl((st)^{m-1}s\\bigr)=\\min(2m-1,1)=1$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "2.2",
            "1.2",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "4.1",
          "claim": "**The longest element.** Suppose $m$ is even and put $w_0:=(st)^{m/2}$. By step 2.2, $\\ell(w_0)=\\min(m,m)=m$. Every rotation $(st)^k$ with $k\\ne m/2$ has $\\ell=\\min(2k,2m-2k)<m$: both terms are even and at most $m$, and equality in either would give $2k=m$ or $2m-2k=m$, that is $k=m/2$. Every reflection $(st)^ks$ with $0\\le k\\le m-1$ has $\\ell=\\min(2k+1,2m-2k-1)\\le m$ by step 3.1, and both entries of that minimum are odd while $m$ is even, so $\\ell\\le m-1<m$. Together with steps 1.1 and 2.1 this shows that $w_0$ is the unique element of length $m$, hence the unique longest element. A reduced word for $w_0$ of length $m$ cannot contain two consecutive equal letters, since deleting that pair would exhibit a shorter word for $w_0$ [F4] in contradiction to $\\ell(w_0)=m$; hence it is alternating. The two alternating words of length $m$ are $(st)^{m/2}$ and $(ts)^{m/2}$, both of which have value $w_0$ because $(ts)^{m/2}=(st)^{-m/2}=(st)^{m/2}$ [F5], and they are reduced by step 1.2; so they are exactly the two reduced expressions of $w_0$. They differ by the single replacement of the alternating block of length $m$ by the other alternating word of the same length, the braid move.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
