    "ex-hh-exchange-deletion-on-a-nonreduced-word": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "1. **Involutions.** $\\sigma_s^2=\\mathrm{id}_E$ for every $s\\in S$; each $\\sigma_s$ is invertible, and $\\ell(s)=1$.\n2. **The rank-two block.** For any distinct $s,t\\in S$, put $m:=m(s,t)$, $c:=c_{st}$, $P:=\\operatorname{span}(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)\\subseteq E$, $A:=\\sigma_s\\sigma_t$ and $B:=A|_P$. Then $A(P)\\subseteq P$, and in the basis $(\\alpha_s,\\alpha_t)$,\n$$B(\\alpha_s)=(c^2-1)\\alpha_s+c\\,\\alpha_t,\\qquad B(\\alpha_t)=-c\\,\\alpha_s-\\alpha_t;\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(B)=c^2-2,\\qquad \\det(B)=1 .$$\nMoreover $A(v)-v\\in P$ for every $v\\in E$. Consequently $E=P\\oplus Q$ with $Q:=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\alpha_u:u\\in S,\\ u\\notin\\{s,t\\}\\}$, and for every $v=p+q$ with $p\\in P$, $q\\in Q$ and every $k\\ge1$,\n$$A^k(p+q)=B^k p+\\bigl(\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{k-1}\\bigr)\\bigl(A(q)-q\\bigr)+q .$$\n3. **Exact order of the rank-two product.** For any distinct $s,t$ and the notation of (2), when $m<\\infty$ put $\\zeta:=\\zeta_{st}$, of order $2m$, so $c=\\zeta+\\zeta^{-1}$; when $m=\\infty$ use $c=2$, with no $\\zeta$ stipulated. Then:\n   (a) if $2<m<\\infty$, the characteristic polynomial of $B$ is $X^2-(c^2-2)X+1=(X-\\zeta^2)(X-\\zeta^{-2})$ with distinct roots; $B$ is diagonalisable, $B^m=\\mathrm{id}_P$, $\\mathrm{id}_P+B+\\cdots+B^{m-1}=0$, and $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for $0<k<m$;\n   (b) if $m=2$, then $c=0$ and $B=-\\mathrm{id}_P$;\n   (c) if $m=\\infty$, then $c=2$, $B\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$, $(B-\\mathrm{id}_P)^2=0$, and $B^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_P$ for every $k\\ge1$.\n   Hence $\\sigma_s\\sigma_t=A$ has order exactly $m$ when $m<\\infty$, and infinite order when $m=\\infty$; in particular $A^m=\\mathrm{id}_E$ and $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_E$ for $0<k<m$ in the finite case.\n4. **The representation and the exact dihedral orders.** The assignment $s\\mapsto\\sigma_s$ respects every defining relator of $W$ and induces a unique homomorphism $\\sigma:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}_K(E)$ with $\\sigma(s)=\\sigma_s$. Consequently, for any distinct $s,t\\in S$, one has $s\\ne t$ in $W$ and $st$ has order exactly $m(s,t)$ in $W$ (infinite when $m(s,t)=\\infty$).\n5. **The signed reflection action.** Let $T:=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}\\subseteq W$. For $s\\in S$ define $U_s:\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T\\to\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ by\n$$U_s(\\varepsilon,r):=\\bigl(\\varepsilon\\cdot(-1)^{\\delta(s,r)},\\ srs\\bigr),$$\nwhere $\\delta(s,r)=1$ if $r=s$ and $\\delta(s,r)=0$ otherwise. Then $U_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$ for every $s$, and the assignment $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot s:=U_s(\\varepsilon,r)$ extends to a well-defined **right action** of $W$ on $\\{\\pm1\\}\\times T$ (so $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot1=(\\varepsilon,r)$ and $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot(wv)=((\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w)\\cdot v$). Indeed the only relations to check are $s^2=1$ and, for distinct $s,t$ with $m=m(s,t)<\\infty$, $(st)^m=1$: $U_s^2=\\mathrm{id}$ because for $r=s$ the two signs cancel while for $r\\ne s$ one has $srs\\ne s$ and $U_s(\\varepsilon,srs)=(\\varepsilon,r)$; and the alternating word $s\\,t\\,s\\,t\\cdots$ of length $2m$ acts trivially because its $2m$ prefix reflections $r_i:=w_{i-1}s_iw_{i-1}^{-1}$ (with $w_j:=s_1\\cdots s_j$) satisfy $r_{i+m}=r_i$ for $1\\le i\\le m$ and $r_1,\\dots,r_m$ are pairwise distinct, so each reflection of the dihedral subgroup $\\langle s,t\\rangle$ occurs exactly twice and every accumulated sign is even while the conjugating coordinate returns to $r$ because $(st)^m=1$.\n6. **Prefix reflections, deletion and expression independence.** Let $(s_1,\\dots,s_k)$ be a word in $S$ with value $w=s_1\\cdots s_k$ and prefix reflections $r_i=s_1\\cdots s_{i-1}s_is_{i-1}\\cdots s_1$, and let $n(r):=\\#\\{i:r_i=r\\}$.\n   (a) If $r_i=r_j$ for some $i<j$, then $s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots\\widehat{s_j}\\cdots s_k=w$: the two letters $s_i,s_j$ can be deleted.\n   (b) $(-1)^{n(r)}=:\\eta(r,w)$ depends only on $w$ and $r$, and the right action satisfies $(\\varepsilon,r)\\cdot w=(\\varepsilon\\,\\eta(r,w),\\,w^{-1}rw)$.\n   (c) If the word is reduced ($k=\\ell(w)$), then $n(r)\\in\\{0,1\\}$ for every $r$, the map $i\\mapsto r_i$ is injective, and the set $\\Phi(w):=\\{r_1,\\dots,r_k\\}=\\{r\\in T:\\eta(r,w)=-1\\}$ is independent of the reduced expression chosen, with $\\#\\Phi(w)=\\ell(w)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $$sw=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_k$$ for some $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,k\\}$; equivalently, $w$ has a reduced expression beginning with $s$, and $s$ is a prefix reflection of any reduced expression of $w$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "ex-hh-finite-dihedral-reduced-words",
          "source_section": "Example",
          "quote": "For $0\\le k\\le m$ one has $$\\ell\\bigl((st)^k\\bigr)=\\min(2k,\\,2(m-k)),$$ and for $0\\le k\\le m-1$ one has $(st)^ks=(ts)^{m-k}s=(ts)^{m-k-1}t$ and $$\\ell\\bigl((st)^ks\\bigr)=\\min(2k+1,\\,2(m-k)-1);$$ in particular $\\ell(s)=1$ and $\\ell\\bigl((st)^{m-1}s\\bigr)=1$.",
          "uses": [
