    "ex-hh-minimal-representatives-for-s2-in-s3": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**Minimal coset representatives.** Every left coset $W_J a:=\\{ua:u\\in W_J\\}$ ($a\\in W$, [[def-coset]]) has a unique element $d$ of minimal length; it is characterized by $\\ell(sd)>\\ell(d)$ for all $s\\in J$, and it satisfies $$\\ell(ud)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)\\qquad\\text{for all }u\\in W_J .$$ Equivalently, every $w\\in W$ has a unique factorization $w=u\\,d$ with $u\\in W_J$ and $d$ the minimal representative of the left coset $W_J d$, and then $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(d)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-coset",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $g\\in G$, the **left coset** and **right coset** of $H$ represented by $g$ are $$gH:=\\{gh:h\\in H\\},\\qquad Hg:=\\{hg:h\\in H\\}.$$ The element $g$ is a **representative** of these cosets.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Consequently $$W_J=\\langle J\\rangle=\\{w\\in W:S(w)\\subseteq J\\}.$$ 2.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "ex-hh-type-a-reduced-words-and-inversions",
          "source_section": "Example",
          "quote": "Then $\\ell(w)=\\operatorname{inv}(\\varphi(w))$ for all $w\\in W$, and the six elements of $W$ and their data are: | $w$ | permutation | length $\\ell(w)$ | inversion number | |---|---|---|---| | $1$ | $\\mathrm{id}$ | $0$ | $0$ | | $s_1$ | $(1\\ 2)$ | $1$ | $1$ | | $s_2$ | $(2\\ 3)$ | $1$ | $1$ | | $s_1s_2$ | $(1\\ 2\\ 3)$ | $2$ | $2$ | | $s_2s_1$ | $(1\\ 3\\ 2)$ | $2$ | $2$ | | $s_1s_2s_1=s_2s_1s_2$ | $(1\\ 3)$ | $3$ | $3$ | Consequently: the words $(s_1,s_2)$, $(s_2,s_1)$ and $(s_1,s_2,s_1)$ are reduced, the two reduced expressions $s_1s_2s_1$ and $s_2s_1s_2$ of the longest element are related by the braid move, and the word $(s_1,s_2,s_1,s_2)$ of length $4$ is nonreduced: it represents $s_2s_1$ (inversion number $2$) and deleting its first and last letters gives the reduced word $(s_2,s_1)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-coset",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $g\\in G$, the **left coset** and **right coset** of $H$ represented by $g$ are $$gH:=\\{gh:h\\in H\\},\\qquad Hg:=\\{hg:h\\in H\\}.$$ The element $g$ is a **representative** of these cosets.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Standard parabolic subgroups.** For $J\\subseteq S$ put $W_J:=\\langle\\{s:s\\in J\\}\\rangle\\le W$ ([[def-generated-subgroup]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-hh-dihedral-root-recurrence-and-root-sign",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$\\sigma_s^2=\\mathrm{id}_E$ for every $s\\in S$; each $\\sigma_s$ is invertible, and $\\ell(s)=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "1.1",
          "claim": "**The parabolic subgroup.** $W_J=\\langle J\\rangle=\\langle s_1\\rangle=\\{1,s_1\\}$: the definition of $W_J$ as the subgroup generated by $J$ is [F5] and its identity with $\\langle s_1\\rangle$ is immediate for $J=\\{s_1\\}$ [F2]; the relator $s_1^2$ of the presentation gives $s_1^2=1$ [F5], so $\\langle s_1\\rangle\\subseteq\\{1,s_1\\}$, and $s_1\\ne1$ because $\\ell(s_1)=1$ [F6], so $\\langle s_1\\rangle$ has the two distinct elements $1,s_1$; it is a group of order $2$, hence isomorphic to $S_2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "1.2",
          "claim": "**The right cosets.** The right cosets $aW_J$ are $\\{1,s_1\\}$, $s_2W_J=\\{s_2,s_2s_1\\}$ and $s_1s_2W_J=\\{s_1s_2,s_1s_2s_1\\}$, disjoint and exhausting $W$ by the six distinct elements listed in [F3]; their minimal elements are $1$ (length $0$), $s_2$ (length $1$, versus $\\ell(s_2s_1)=2$) and $s_1s_2$ (length $2$, versus $\\ell(s_1s_2s_1)=3$), unique by the right-coset half of [F1]. For these three elements $d$ the right-handed additivity of [F1] gives $\\ell(ds_1)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(s_1)=\\ell(d)+1$, namely $\\ell(s_1)=1$, $\\ell(s_2s_1)=2=1+1$ and $\\ell(s_1s_2s_1)=3=2+1$, as asserted; equivalently the right coset representatives satisfy the characterisation $\\ell(ds_1)>\\ell(d)$ of [F1].",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "2.1",
          "claim": "**The left cosets and their minimal elements.** For $W_J=\\{1,s_1\\}$ the sets $W_Ja=\\{ua:u\\in W_J\\}$ of [F4], with $a\\in W$, can be enumerated using the six-element list of [F3] they are $W_J\\cdot1=\\{1,s_1\\}$, $W_Js_2=\\{s_2,s_1s_2\\}$ and $W_Js_2s_1=\\{s_2s_1,s_1s_2s_1\\}$, which are disjoint and exhaust $W$. Reading the lengths off [F3], the minimum of $\\ell$ is $0$ on $\\{1,s_1\\}$ (attained at $1$), $1$ on $\\{s_2,s_1s_2\\}$ (attained at $s_2$) and $2$ on $\\{s_2s_1,s_1s_2s_1\\}$ (attained at $s_2s_1$); by [F1] each coset has a unique element of minimal length, so the minimal representatives are $1$, $s_2$ and $s_2s_1$ with lengths $0$, $1$ and $2$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
